[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known, with one fix queued. It held two problems, and that
fix reached only one of them. Each got its own ticket. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October, and
the two problems ended differently: `parser` is fixed, and `hint` is won't fix.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table's last column compares each cause with #4471 only. The cause it calls "no ticket of
its own" now has one, below.

## The two problems

**`parser`: [ticket #1](https://github.com/kacputer/kb-4471-split/issues/1).** The shared parser
reads every date month first, and these tenants write the day first. The dates come out flipped.
**Fixed:** `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

**`hint`: [ticket #2](https://github.com/kacputer/kb-4471-split/issues/2).** Under its date field,
`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY`. People type month first as told, and the form reads the date the
tenant's way, day first. These reports come only through the form, from tenants on `booking-v3`.
The parser fix did not change this. **Won't fix:** engineering retired the `booking-v3` form.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix did not resolve the <code>hint</code> reports. The problem ends only because the form is retired, so
do not count these reports as fixed by the parser change.
</Callout>

## How to tell them apart

Look up the tenant, then ask how the report came in. The subject does not help, because both
causes share subject lines. Neither does the intake tag: it is a guess from the reporter's
browser, and every `en-US` browser was tagged `hint`, whatever the tenant runs.

<DedupeExplorer />

## What this does not settle

The note under the table lists reports the desk read as a one-day move. They are counted where the
tenant places them, but a flipped day and month does not explain a one-day move. Nobody has
checked them against a cause, and neither outcome above shows they are resolved. If reports like these
keep coming, open a new ticket for them.

The export overlaps, so some reports appear twice. The table counts each id once.

